{# Footer: compliance / unsubscribe line. #}
<mj-section padding="8px 32px 24px 32px">
  <mj-column>
    <mj-divider border-width="1px" border-color="{$ tokens.color.divider $}" padding="0 0 16px 0" />
    {# The opt-out clause is rendered for MARKETING campaigns only — `meta.category` decides, which is
       what that field is for. A transactional receipt must NOT offer to unsubscribe from receipts,
       and offering it would be wrong rather than merely redundant. build/validate-email.ts asserts
       the other direction: a marketing email that ends up WITHOUT {{unsubscribe}} fails the build.

       Within a marketing send the clause is still wrapped in a Handlebars if-conditional (passed
       through the build untouched): the sending system renders it only when it provides an
       `unsubscribe` value (its unsubscribe toggle on), and drops it otherwise. The `#if` open is
       emitted via a Nunjucks string because Nunjucks' comment token is `{&hash;` — the two-brace
       `#if` would otherwise be misread as a comment start; the close and the href are plain
       passthrough. The .unsub-sim span lets the gallery preview simulate on/off (preview-samples.ts
       fills the sample so it shows by default; the gallery checkbox toggles it). #}
    <mj-text font-size="12px" color="{$ tokens.color.footer $}" line-height="1.6" padding="0">
      {$ content.compliance.sourceBasis $}
      {% if meta.category == 'marketing' %}
      {$ '{{#if unsubscribe}}' $}<span class="unsub-sim">Prefer not to receive these emails? <a href="{{unsubscribe}}" style="color:{$ tokens.color.footer $};text-decoration:underline;">Unsubscribe</a>.</span>{{/if}}
      {% endif %}
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
